89]],"13209":[["堺村",0,1889],["忠生村",0,1889],["鶴川村",0,1889],["南村",0,1889]],"13210":[["小金井村",0,1889]],"13211":[["小平村",0,1889]],"13212":[["日野町",0,1893],["七生村",0,1889]],"13213":[["東村山村",0,1889]],"13214":[["国分寺村",0,1889]],"13215":[["谷保村",0,1889]],"13218":[["福生村",0,1889],["熊川村",0,1889]],"13219":[["狛江村",0,1889]],"13221":[["清瀬村",0,1889]],"13222":[["久留米村",0,1889]],"13224":[["多摩村",0,1889]],"13225":[["稲城村",0,1889]],"13227":[["西多摩村",0,1889]],"13228":[["小宮村",0,1889],["五日市町",0,1889],["菅生村",0,1889],["草花村",0,1889],["増戸村",0,1889],["戸倉村",0,1889],["瀬戸岡村",0,1889],["原小宮村",0,1889],["東秋留村",0,1889],["西秋留村",0,1889]],"13229":[["保谷村",0,1889],["田無町",0,1889]],"13303":[["箱根ケ崎村",0,1889],["長岡村",0,1889],["石畑村",0,1889],["殿ケ谷村",0,1889]],"13305":[["大久野村",0,1889],["平井村",0,1889]],"13307":[["檜原村",0,1889]],"13308":[["氷川村",0,1889],["古里村",0,1889],["小河内村",0,1889]],"13361":[["岡田村",0,1908],["元村",0,1908],["泉津村",0,1908],["野増村",0,1908],["差木地村",0,1908],["波浮港村",0,1908]],"13401":[["三根村",0,1908],["大賀郷村",0,1908],["末吉村",0,1908],["樫立村",0,1908],["中之郷村",0,1908]]},"heisei":{"13229":[["保谷市",0,2001],["田無市",0,2001]]},"yurai":{"13":{"新宿区":"From Naitō-Shinjuku, a new post town opened on the Kōshū road in 1698 on land of the Naitō lords of Takatō.","江東区":"\"East of the river\" — east of the Sumida; chosen over Tatsumi, Higashi and Eitai.","大田区":"One character each from Ōmori and Kamata, the two wards merged in 1947.","杉並区":"From the row of cedars planted along the Ōme road by the lord of Narimune and Tabata villages in the early Edo period.","板橋区":"From the \"plank bridge\" carrying the old Nakasendō over the Shakujii River.","練馬区":"Several theories: Nerima Castle; a \"neriba\" where red loam was kneaded; or the Nara-period post station Norinuma.","足立区":"From Adachi district of Musashi, set up after the Taika reforms.","葛飾区":"Katsushika was the name of a wide district of Shimōsa, already in the Man'yōshū; this area itself was called Kasai until early modern times.","江戸川区":"From the Edo River on its eastern edge; \"Matsue-ku\", after the planned ward office, was considered first.","八王子市":"From the Hachiōji shrine and Hachiōji Castle named after it.","町田市":"Theories: \"machi\" as a block of paddy; a busy market (machi = ichi); or \"matsurida\", fields kept for the market god.","北区":"When Ōji and Takinogawa wards merged, the candidates included Jōhoku, Asukayama and Akabane. Asukayama was the favourite but thought hard to read, so the plain compass name Kita (\"north\") won.","豊島区":"When four towns of Kita-Toshima District joined Tokyo City they took the district name, which goes back to ancient Toshima District in Musashi. The city first proposed Ikebukuro, then Mejiro, before petitions settled on Toshima.","墨田区":"Takes \"sumi\" (墨) from Bokutei, the popular name of the Sumida riverbank, and \"da\" from the Sumida River. Most people wanted 隅田, but 隅 was not on the official list of characters for general use.","府中市":"Fuchū means \"seat of the provincial government\": the capital of ancient Musashi Province stood here.","港区":"The shortlist narrowed to Jōnan and Higashi-minato (\"east port\", for the Port of Tokyo). \"Tōkyō-to Higashi-minato-ku\" repeated sounds, so the \"east\" was dropped, leaving Minato.","調布市":"Under the ancient tax system the \"chō\" levy was paid in local produce — here, cloth (fu).","文京区":"An auspicious coinage from the merger of Koishikawa and Hongō wards: short, easy to write, and fitting the area's image as a centre of learning (bunkyō). Keihoku, Kasuga and Yushima were also proposed.","荒川区":"Named after the Arakawa River. Since 1965, when the Arakawa Drainage Channel became the official main stream, the river through the ward has been the Sumida — but the name stayed. Tokyo City had first proposed Mikawashima, the planned site of the ward office.","台東区":"Shitaya and Asakusa could not agree (Ueno versus Higashi). The governor proposed \"Taitō\", already used by a Shitaya primary school: \"tai\" for the Ueno heights and \"tō\" for the old town to their east.","西東京市":"When Tanashi and Hōya merged, a public call produced five finalists — Nishi-Tōkyō, Keyakino, Kita-Tama, Hibari and Midorino — and a residents' survey picked Nishi-Tōkyō.","小平市":"Coined at the 1889 merger of seven villages: \"ko\" from Ogawa, the main village, and \"taira\" for the flat land of the Musashino plateau.","日野市":"Uncertain. One Edo gazetteer says the provincial capital's signal-fire post stood here (\"tobuhino\", fire field); another records the Nishi band's founder Himatsuri Muneyori enshrining a Hino deity.","立川市":"Two theories: seen from the provincial capital, the Tama River ran \"upright\" (tate) past the east–west Tama hills, giving \"tate no kawa\"; or the local Tachikawa clan built a fort at today's Fusai-ji.","中央区":"At the geographic centre of old Tokyo City and its business heart — the first place in Japan officially named \"Chūō\" (central). Edo, Ginza and Ōedo were also proposed.","東村山市":"The eastern end of Murayama in Musashi — home of the Murayama warrior band, at the southern foot of the Sayama hills, which were called \"mureyama\" (massed hills).","青梅市":"Best known is the legend of Masakado's vow: Taira no Masakado planted his plum-branch riding whip at Kongō-ji; it took root and bore fruit that stayed green (ao) and never fell, even past summer.","小金井市":"Either Kanai-hara, the land south of the \"hake\" escarpment, read as \"Koganei-hara\"; or the springs along the escarpment, worth their weight in gold — \"the golden well\".","東久留米市":"From Kurume River, the old name of the Kurome River in the north of the city. Since Kurume in Fukuoka already existed, the new city took the familiar station name, Higashi-Kurume (\"East Kurume\").","狛江市":"From the old township of Komae (its own origin is debated). At city status 73.5% of residents voted to keep the name; some wanted it in hiragana.","あきる野市":"From the old names 秋留 and 阿伎留 (Akiru), and Akiru Shrine in old Itsukaichi, written in hiragana. Akigawa proposed \"Akiruno\"; Itsukaichi wanted \"Akigawa-Itsukaichi\", but gave way in 1995.","清瀬市":"One character each from the local place Kiyoto (where Yamato Takeru, resting under a tree, is said to have praised the \"clean earth\") and the Yanase River — or simply the river's \"clear shallows\".","国立市":"A new station between Kokubunji and Tachikawa on the Chūō Line took one character from each: Kunitachi. Because it reads like kokuritsu (\"national\"), it is sometimes written in hiragana."}},"kyu":{"13101":{"t":1,"k":5,"r":[["@jisha",5]]},"13103":{"t":16,"k":2096,"r":[["@bakufu",2021],["@jisha",75]]},"13104":{"t":23,"k":3580,"r":[["@hatamoto",1300],["@bakufu",1225],["@jisha",1056]]},"13105":{"t":5,"k":369,"r":[["@jisha",232],["@bakufu",113],["@hatamoto",24]]},"13106":{"t":31,"k":2081,"r":[["@bakufu",1087],["@jisha",995]]},"13107":{"t":12,"k":3664,"r":[["@bakufu",3311],["@jisha",353]]},"13108":{"t":28,"k":6271,"r":[["@bakufu",5956],["一橋家領",244],["@jisha",72]]},"13109":{"t":13,"k":5466,"r":[["@bakufu",5331],["@jisha",134],["@other",1]]},"13110":{"t":6,"k":2564,"r":[["@bakufu",1246],["@jisha",1050],["@hatamoto",268]]},"13111":{"t":49,"k":17655,"r":[["@bakufu",12166],["@jisha",4064],["@hatamoto",1425]]},"13112":{"t":43,"k":10582,"r":[["@bakufu",5582],["Hikone Domain",2211],["@hatamoto",1989],["@jisha",800]]},"13113":{"t":19,"k":3003,"r":[["@bakufu",1530],["@jisha",915],["@hatamoto",559]]},"13114":{"t":12,"k":4816,"r":[["@bakufu",3020],["@hatamoto",1759],["@jisha",36],["@other",1]]},"13115":{"t":20,"k":7140,"r":[["@bakufu",5044],["@hatamoto",1340],["@jisha",756]]},"13116":{"t":10,"k":2375,"r":[["@bakufu",1118],["@jisha",715],["@hatamoto",541],["@other",1]]},"13117":{"t":16,"k":7574,"r":[["@jisha",4868],["@hatamoto",1443],["@bakufu",1263]]},"13118":{"t":12,"k":4604,"r":[["@jisha",4150],["@bakufu",438],["@hatamoto",15],["@other",1]]},"13119":{"t":18,"k":12509,"r":[["@bakufu",8692],["@jisha",2522],["@hatamoto",1295]]},"13120":{"t":15,"k":13538,"r":[["@bakufu",12533],["Ryūgasaki Domain",732],["@hatamoto",201],["@jisha",71],